<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!--
      In a high-contrast theme the palette is not ours. Windows forces four or five colours and
      we do not get to mean anything with them: green-is-whole and amber-is-not are gone. This
      works because health was never encoded only in colour — it is encoded in shape.

      Every fill goes transparent, with ONE exception - WlCard, which backs the dialogs and the
      tray menu, and those are layered windows with nothing opaque behind them. See its comment.
      Nothing here is a literal.

      The keys are SystemColors.*ColorKey, not SystemColors.*Color: the latter is the Color
      VALUE, and a value used as a DynamicResource key never resolves — the brush would render
      black in high contrast, which is the one theme where that is unsurvivable.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WlBg"     Color="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.WindowColorKey}}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WlChrome" Color="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.WindowColorKey}}" />
    <!--
      WlCard IS OPAQUE, and it is the one surface that has to be.

      "Every fill goes transparent" is a rule about surfaces drawn ON something - a card inside
      the main window sits on WlBg, so dropping its fill reveals WindowColor and the card is
      still legible, separated by its border exactly as the high-contrast spec intends.

      A dialog card is not that. Every dialog here is a layered window - WindowStyle=None,
      AllowsTransparency=True, Background=Transparent - with WlScrim edge to edge and the card
      centred on top. In high contrast WlScrim is transparent too (a dialog is separated by a
      border, not by dimming), so a transparent WlCard leaves the dialog with NO opaque pixel
      anywhere: the desktop shows through the card, behind its own text. The tray menu is the
      same shape for the same reason - a popup is its own window.

      So this key resolves to WindowColor rather than Transparent. Inside the main window that
      is visually identical to transparent, because WlBg is WindowColor as well; on a layered
      window it is the difference between a dialog and a hole.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WlCard"   Color="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.WindowColorKey}}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WlRaised" Color="Transparent" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WlSunken" Color="Transparent" />

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WlText"   Color="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.WindowTextColorKey}}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WlStrong" Color="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.WindowTextColorKey}}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WlMuted"  Color="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.GrayTextColorKey}}" />

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WlLine"   Color="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.WindowTextColorKey}}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WlLine2"  Color="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.WindowTextColorKey}}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WlHover"  Color="Transparent" />

    <!-- The high-contrast spec: "Hover = 1px HotTrack outline, no fill." WlHover goes transparent
         above, so there is no other feedback left without this. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WlHotTrack" Color="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.HotTrackColorKey}}" />

    <!-- Red and the accent are gone. Primary = Highlight; nothing here is red. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WlAccent"     Color="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.HighlightColorKey}}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WlAccentInk"  Color="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.HighlightTextColorKey}}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WlAccentSoft" Color="Transparent" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WlAccentLine" Color="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.WindowTextColorKey}}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WlDanger"     Color="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.WindowTextColorKey}}" />
    <!-- No tint in high contrast - every fill goes transparent (the high-contrast spec). The Failed
         strip separates by its border and glyph, not by a fill. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WlDangerSoft" Color="Transparent" />

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WlOk"       Color="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.WindowTextColorKey}}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WlOkSoft"   Color="Transparent" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WlWarn"     Color="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.WindowTextColorKey}}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WlWarnSoft" Color="Transparent" />

    <!-- A dialog is separated by a border, not by dimming: dimming is opacity. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WlScrim"    Color="Transparent" />

    <!-- Genuinely transparent in every theme: the caption row when Mica has taken. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WlTransparent" Color="Transparent" />

    <!-- The close caption button's hover fill. Highlight, not WlDanger's WindowText - nothing in
         high contrast is red (the high-contrast spec), and a close button still needs to visibly
         react on hover. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WlCaptionCloseHover"
                      Color="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.HighlightColorKey}}" />

</ResourceDictionary>
